WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.710 --> 00:04.740
Mindsets are attitudes and beliefs
that your brain uses as filters.

2
00:05.480 --> 00:10.400
Think of them as a mental lens that impacts
how you see yourself and the world around you.

3
00:11.210 --> 00:14.240
You perceive something, a conversation or an event,

4
00:14.660 --> 00:20.670
and your mindset influences your thoughts and feelings
and helps you interpret them and define your choices.

5
00:21.450 --> 00:25.060
More than impacting how you see, think,
and feel about things,

6
00:25.410 --> 00:28.070
your mindsets ultimately drive your behavior.

7
00:28.580 --> 00:33.790
For example, let's say your colleague asks you
to sketch a visual design for a brochure.

8
00:34.470 --> 00:37.760
If your first thought is,
“I'm just not good at that sort of thing,”

9
00:38.210 --> 00:40.480
you may be operating from a fixed mindset,

10
00:40.900 --> 00:44.610
and the task at hand might make you feel
anxious or even annoyed.

11
00:45.380 --> 00:48.160
It could lead you to procrastinating
and avoiding the job.

12
00:49.190 --> 00:52.030
Can you think about a time
you held a fixed mindset recently?

13
00:54.210 --> 00:55.970
What impact did this have on you?

14
00:58.280 --> 01:01.860
The next time you find yourself
in a similar situation and mindset,

15
01:02.250 --> 01:03.750
you can choose a different approach.

16
01:04.130 --> 01:06.790
If you try out a growth mindset, such as

17
01:07.140 --> 01:09.670
“I welcome new opportunities to grow my skills,”

18
01:10.090 --> 01:13.630
you're more likely to be excited and curious
about this new challenge.

19
01:14.470 --> 01:19.230
In any given moment, the mindset you operate from
can either be a deliberate choice,

20
01:19.590 --> 01:22.690
or one of the many default mindsets
each of us carries.

21
01:23.690 --> 01:25.920
But where do these default mindsets come from?

22
01:26.600 --> 01:32.550
Typically, they are shaped by past experiences
and the reactions you've had in similar situations.

23
01:33.320 --> 01:38.080
As a result, you carry helpful mindsets
and some not-so-helpful ones.

24
01:39.110 --> 01:43.750
Maybe a manager in the past consistently praised you
for being an outside-the-box thinker,

25
01:44.290 --> 01:50.020
so you're wired to prefer a mindset of experimentation
and risk-taking in similar environments.

26
01:50.980 --> 01:58.400
Or maybe you worked at an organization where you were frequently
made to feel inadequate when you voiced suggestions during team meetings.

27
01:59.010 --> 02:03.330
In turn, this instilled a mindset
that you must be an expert to speak up,

28
02:03.810 --> 02:06.470
that otherwise you may look bad at your job.

29
02:07.080 --> 02:12.290
Take a moment to think which mindsets you might be carrying
that are not actually serving you well.

30
02:14.660 --> 02:17.920
Just because you're carrying all these
default mindsets with you,

31
02:18.280 --> 02:20.830
it doesn't mean you always have to operate from them.

32
02:21.610 --> 02:28.600
In fact, you can actually choose the mindset that is most appropriate at the time,
depending on what the situation is and what you want to accomplish.

33
02:28.600 --> 02:36.800
Research shows it's possible if you're aware of what your current mindset is
and know which one might serve you better instead.

34
02:37.450 --> 02:38.690
How important is this?

35
02:39.170 --> 02:40.190
Extremely.

36
02:40.650 --> 02:43.200
Mindsets can empower you or hold you back.

37
02:43.780 --> 02:53.030
Study after study confirms that everything from school achievement and job performance
to life satisfaction, stress management, and even your physical health

38
02:53.480 --> 02:56.710
is driven at least in part by your mindsets.

39
02:57.670 --> 03:00.640
Now that you're aware of the abundance
of mindsets around you,

40
03:00.970 --> 03:03.460
why not choose the ones that serve you best?
